## Why This Is Asked
Tests bitboard reasoning, edge handling, and efficient parallelism using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Bitboard edge wrapping and file masks
- Parallel neighbor computation via multiple shifts
- Safe, branch-free bitwise design

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t neighborMask64(uint64_t board) {
    const uint64_t notA = 0xfefefefefefefefeULL;
    const uint64_t notH = 0x7f7f7f7f7f7f7f7fULL;
    uint64_t east  = (board & notH) << 1;
    uint64_t west  = (board & notA) >> 1;
    uint64_t south = board << 8;
    uint64_t north = board >> 8;
    uint64_t ne = (board & notH) >> 7;
    uint64_t nw = (board & notA) >> 9;
    uint64_t se = (board & notH) << 9;
    uint64_t sw = (board & notA) << 7;
    return east | west | south | north | ne | nw | se | sw;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a 32-bit board layout?
- How would you unit-test edge cases (corners and edges) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of efficient bit-hacks and how to compute parity without loops or branching, which is common in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- Bit folding using XOR and shifts to aggregate bit information
- Constant-time parity methods and small lookup tables
- Branchless implementation for predictable performance

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t parity64(uint64_t x) {
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return (0x6996 >> (x & 0xF)) & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit values?
- Compare with using builtin parity intrinsics in terms of portability and performance.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary extraction and 128-bit layouts; practical in serialization, bitboard ops, or crypto.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit extraction
- Masking and shifts
- Boundary checks

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t extractWord128(uint128 x, unsigned pos){
  switch(pos){
    case 0: return (uint32_t)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    case 1: return (uint32_t)((x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    case 2: return (uint32_t)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    default: return (uint32_t)((x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to return 16-bit or 64-bit words?
- Can you refactor to be branchless while preserving correctness?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to implement a classic bit-twiddling technique (popcount) on 128-bit integers without branching. This skill is useful in hash tables, connectivity algorithms, and low-latency code.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting (divide-and-conquer approach)
- Splitting a 128-bit value into two 64-bit chunks
- Branchless arithmetic using bitwise masks

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount64(uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
    return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Transposition in bit-sliced form is a classic challenge that tests mastery of layout tricks and how data moves across a 128-bit container without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice layout and Morton-order ideas
- Multi-stage bit redistribution with masks
- Branchless, vector-style bit manipulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline for guidance
uint128 transpose8x16(uint128 x) {
  // stage 1: swap adjacent bits
  x = (x & 0x5555...5555) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555...5555);
  // stage 2: swap pairs
  x = (x & 0x3333...3333) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333...3333);
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x & 0x0F0F...0F0F) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F...0F0F);
  // stage 4: move bytes
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) bit counting and handling of 128-bit data without branching.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR-based popcount
- 128-bit data split into 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling tricks

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned pop64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (unsigned)(v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize with SIMD intrinsics on x86 or ARM?
- How would you unit-test edge cases: all zeros, all ones, randoms?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to scale classic SWAR nibble-swaps to 128-bit values, ensuring implementations stay constant-time and border-safe.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit lane-wise bit-twiddling
- SWAR-style mask-and-shift patterns
- Safe handling of hi/lo halves

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 nibbleSwap128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = ((x.lo & M) << 4) | ((x.lo >> 4) & M);
  r.hi = ((x.hi & M) << 4) | ((x.hi >> 4) & M);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 256-bit types? 
- How do you test with all-zero and all-0xFF inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reduce a 128-bit input to a single bit using only shifts and XORs, a common low-level optimization pattern. It emphasizes branchless, predictable timing.

## Key Concepts
- XOR folding to accumulate parity across halves
- Branchless, constant-time bitwise manipulation
- Handling 128-bit input via hi/lo halves without loops

## Code Example
```javascript
function parity128(hi, lo) {
  let p = hi ^ lo;
  p = p ^ (p >>> 32);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 16);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 8);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 4);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 2);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 1);
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 256-bit values?
- What's the performance impact of using XOR vs. addition for parity?
- Can you implement this using only addition and subtraction?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness handling and 128-bit manipulation with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit byte reversal
- 128-bit composition
- Branchless bit-twiddling

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x) {
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}
uint128 mirrorBytes128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 y; y.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo); y.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi); return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for streaming data where only a portion is available?
- What are the performance trade-offs of inlining reverseBytes64?

## Why This Is Asked

- Demonstrates ability to replace branches with bit-twiddling for performance.
- Applies a well-known, portable technique to a common memory-allocation problem.
- Handles edge cases and overflow without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix-fill trick to propagate the highest set bit.
- Branchless arithmetic to compute next power of two.
- Overflow behavior on 32-bit boundaries.

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t nextPowerOfTwo32(uint32_t n) {
  // Assumes n > 0. No explicit branches.
  n--;
  n |= n >> 1;
  n |= n >> 2;
  n |= n >> 4;
  n |= n >> 8;
  n |= n >> 16;
  return n + 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does this behave for n = 0x80000000 or 0xFFFFFFFF?
- Can you adapt this to 64-bit inputs with no loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply fixed-pattern bit-twiddling without loops, focusing on nibble-level reversal. It shows understanding of masks and shifts across 64 bits.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise masks across 64 bits
- Fixed-sequence shifts for in-place reversal
- No branching ensures predictable performance

## Code Example
```c
uint64 nibbleReverse64(uint64 x){
  x = ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  x = ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  x = ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit values?
- Compare with a small LUT approach in terms of size and cache behavior.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of bit-twiddling tricks for 128-bit values without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR/popcount for 64-bit chunks
- Safe 128-bit aggregation without branches

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  auto count64 = [](uint64_t v){
    v = v - ((v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v>>8);
    v = v + (v>>16);
    v = v + (v>>32);
    return (uint64_t)(v & 0x7F);
  };
  uint64_t c1 = count64(hi);
  uint64_t c2 = count64(lo);
  return (uint32_t)(c1 + c2);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit value?
- What are the risks of using this in performance-critical hot paths compared to builtin intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of compact bit-twiddling techniques to count bits without loops, a common interview staple for performance-critical code.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit counting (SIMD-style within a register)
- Bit masks and shifts for aggregation
- Portability across unsigned integers and compilers

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t n) {
  n = n - ((n >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (n * 0x01010101) >> 24;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 64-bit?  
- What tests would you add to verify edge cases (all zeros, all ones, random patterns)?
